My initial look at the guidlines leaves me puzzled. They seem to rule out
articles pegged at the neophytes, but I've seen other articles in the
Developer that were old hat for the journeymen among your readers. While my
proposed article might have some value even for those with REXX experience, it
is primarily pegged at those who are just learning it. Does that put it out of
scope?

I.    Introduction

      A.  What is REXX

      B.  Summary of pitfalls

II.   Specific examples and recommended avoidance tactics

      A.  Constants

      B.  Continuation

      C.  Lables and signal

      D.  PROC

      E.  Variable references

III.  Recap


I also have a couple of policy questions. I would like to submit the same, or
a similar, article to the National Systems Programmers' Association (NaSPA)
and to permit its dissemination by nonprofit BBSs and user groups. They are
willing to allow this (in fact they did, for my prior article), but I have no
idea what M-F regards as negotiable and what is set in concrete.


